   "markdown": {
    "text": "Distributed Fibre Sensing of Soil Moisture\n\nThe transect map in the appendix is printed in landscape orientation so that the whole 120 m section fits on one page at a legible scale. Each marker on the map shows the position of a reference probe, and the shading gives the mean water content of the surrounding five metres of fibre during the dry season. Probe readings and fibre readings agree well on the sandy section, while the clayey section shows a systematic offset of about two percent in water content.\n\nWe attribute the offset to the slower dielectric response of bound water in the clay, which the present calibration does not treat separately; a two-phase correction is left for future work.\n\nData and analysis scripts are archived in the project repository\n\n[1] A. Novak and L. Petrov, Distributed strain sensing in unsaturated soils, J. Geotech. Sens. 14 (2021) 233-248.\n[2] M. Ibarra, Temperature compensation in fibre interrogators, Meas. Sci. Eng. 7 (2019) 55-61.\n[3] S. Lindqvist et al., Dielectric mixing models for field soils, Soil Phys. Rev. 31 (2020) 1-19.\n\n[4] T. Okoro, Calibration of buried optical sensors, Proc. Sensor Syst. Conf. (2022) 402-409.\n\n[5] R. Haas, Long-term drift of fibre interrogators, Appl. Opt. Sens. 5 (2018) 90-97.\n\nNorthbridge Geosensing Group | Technical report series\n\n3",
